Pattern:

Traversal + Two Pointers

// Class to hold the solution logic
class Solution {
    // Function to remove duplicates from sorted array in-place
    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        // If array is empty, return 0
        if (nums.length == 0) return 0;

        // Pointer for last unique element
        int i = 0;

        // Start from second element
        for (int j = 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
            // If new unique element is found
            if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {
                // Move unique position forward
                i++;
                // Place new unique element
                nums[i] = nums[j];
            }
        }

        // i is last index of unique element, count = i + 1
        return i + 1;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {0,0,1,1,1,2,2,3,3,4};

        Solution sol = new Solution();
        int k = sol.removeDuplicates(nums);

        System.out.println("Unique count = " + k);
        System.out.print("Array after removing duplicates: ");
        for (int x = 0; x < k; x++) {
            System.out.print(nums[x] + " ");
        }
    }
}




Step 4 : The Biggest Idea

We'll use two pointers.

Slow Pointer (i)

Points to the last unique element.

Fast Pointer (j)

Scans the array looking for the next unique value.
Loop Invariant (The Secret Used by Top Programmers)

This is the most important part.

After every iteration:

The subarray from index 0 to i contains all unique elements in sorted order.

This Is the Secret

The slow pointer is NOT finding duplicates.

It is marking:

"Where should I place the next unique element?"

The fast pointer asks:

"Is this element unique?"

If yes:

Move Slow

↓

Copy Fast Value

↓

Continue

Step 1: Pattern Recognition

When you first read:

Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array

Your brain should immediately say:

Sorted Array
        ↓
Duplicates are Adjacent
        ↓
Two Pointers

Notice the word sorted.

Without sorting, this algorithm doesn't work.

Step 2: What Do the Pointers Mean?

Many beginners say:

i and j

Professionals say:

Pointer	Meaning
i	Write Pointer (last unique element)
j	Read Pointer (scans every element)

I don't call them slow and fast anymore.

I call them

Write Pointer

Read Pointer

because that's exactly what they do.
